q9
==

`./q9` starts this tree under QEMU and boots Glenda's normal graphical
TaijiOS profile.

Examples:

	./q9
	./q9 --text
	./q9 tty
	./q9 --smp 2
	./q9 --gpu vmware
	./q9 --kernel pc
	./q9 --kernel /sys/src/9/pc/9pc
	./q9 --rebuild-desktop
	./q9 acme
	./q9 rill
	./q9 run 'rill'
	./q9 tty-run 'pwd; ls /sys/src/cmd'
	./q9 run 'cd /sys/src/cmd/rill && mk install'
	make driver-smoke
	./q9 --raw

Use `./q9 tty`, `./q9 shell`, or `./q9 --text` when you want the q9
shell-first profile.  That profile leaves you at `term%` on the serial
stdio console instead of starting the window system at boot.

From the TaijiOS prompt in tty mode, run `startwm`, `rill`,
`acme`, or any other command normally.  Use
`./q9 rill` or `./q9 acme` when you want a graphical QEMU
display from startup.  The `startwm` helper starts `rill` by default:

	startwm
	startwm rill
	startwm acme

The start helpers do not replace the shell, so exiting the graphical
program returns to the same tty session.

The q9 PXE profile also sets `nousbboot=1` and `q9fast=1`, which skip
boot and terminal startup work that is slow and not useful for this VM,
such as USB probing, disk probing, DNS, timesync, and APM.

Graphical q9 boots use the installed desktop binaries by default to keep
startup fast.  Use `./q9 --rebuild-desktop` or set `Q9_BUILD_DESKTOP=1`
when you want q9 to run `mk install` for Kryon, t9, Shelf, and Rill
before launching the desktop.

`make driver-smoke` boots the PXE q9 profile, checks the expected virtual
network, source mount, and installed desktop binaries, then reports elapsed
time and key device lines in `boot/q9/driver-smoke.log`.

Run `q9hw` inside TaijiOS to print the detected drivers, network, storage,
display/input devices, desktop binaries, and q9 boot flags.  The driver smoke
test runs the same command, so new driver work has a stable guest-side report
to compare against.

The default and verified q9 NIC is `virtio-net-pci`.  Use
`--net-device model` or `Q9_NET_DEVICE=model` to probe another QEMU NIC
without editing the launcher.  q9 also exports `TMPDIR`, defaulting to `/tmp`,
so QEMU snapshot scratch files do not depend on a writable `/var/tmp`.
If your QEMU build still writes snapshot scratch files under `/var/tmp`, set
`Q9_COPY_IMAGE=1`; q9 will copy the boot image into `$TMPDIR` and run that
temporary copy without QEMU's snapshot mode.

The default graphical adapter is QEMU's standard VGA path using TaijiOS'
VESA framebuffer setup.  Use `--gpu vmware` or `Q9_GPU=vmware` to boot
with QEMU's VMware SVGA adapter and the matching q9 PXE profile.  This
uses TaijiOS' existing VMware SVGA framebuffer driver; it is not a
virtio-gpu or 3D acceleration path.

Use `--kernel name` or `Q9_KERNEL=name` to boot a different installed 386
kernel.  For example, `--kernel pc` resolves to `ether0!/386/9pc`, and
`--kernel pcdisk` resolves to `ether0!/386/9pcdisk`.  Explicit boot paths
are accepted too, such as `--kernel /sys/src/9/pc/9pc`.  q9 implements this
with a temporary generated PXE profile, so the normal tracked text and GUI
profiles stay unchanged.

The q9 profiles set `q9unix=1`, which enables small shell conveniences in
Glenda's profile: `ll`, `la`, `l`, `cdup`, `clear`, `cls`, `path`, and
`which`.  It also appends APE tools to `/bin`, exposes `git/compat` as
`git`, and adds helpers for web downloads.

Run `q9setup` inside TaijiOS to see the bundled development tools.  APE,
git, SSH, hget/webfs, archive tools, Ghostscript/page, VNC, and common
text tools are already present in this tree.

Run `q9deps` inside TaijiOS for optional internet-backed installs.  For
example, `q9deps go-bootstrap` downloads the official plan9/386 Go
bootstrap archive, unpacks it under `/tmp`, binds its `bin` directory into
`/bin`, and writes `$home/lib/go.rc` so future sessions can find it.  Use
`q9deps clone url [dir]` to clone external git repositories with the
bundled git wrapper.

Use `--smp n` or `Q9_SMP=n` to expose multiple CPUs to QEMU.  q9 keeps
TaijiOS MP disabled with `*nomp=1` for now because this PC kernel can panic
under QEMU during LAPIC clock calibration.  This keeps normal boot stable.

Use `--raw` when you want to see the BIOS/kernel boot log or when running
without a real terminal.
